hich way it  ultimately develops  coming up on long crime daily three men  convicted in the murder of unarmed black  man ahmad arbury are sentenced in  federal court plus a father of one of  the victims of the parkland school  shooting sits down with law and crime  amid a break in the gunman's penalty  phase trial  [Music]  [Music]  and welcome back the three men convicted  in the 2020 shooting death of ahmad  arbory are sentenced in federal court  father and son gregory and travis  mcmichael and their neighbor william  roddy bryan were previously convicted in  state court and sentenced to life in  prison for the murder of a mod arbory  this after they armed themselves to  chase down the unarmed black man  assuming he was a burglar a federal jury  in february found mcmichaels and brian  killed arbory because of his race they  were also found guilty of federal  kidnapping charges attorneys assigned to  the defendants previously requested  their clients be sentenced to federal  prison for their own safety all three  men are already serving life sentences  for their convictions in state court on  monday travis and gregory were both  given second life sentences plus ten and  seven years respectively for their  federal hate crimes convictions and  brian was sentenced to 35 years late  monday afternoon  so joe i guess the question is what is  the difference between being locked up  in federal prison versus state prison  namely it tends to be safer you're more  likely to deal with white-collar  criminals folks that are less violent  etc so you add that along with the  notion that in georgia things would be  very hot as it were  with them being in state prison and they  would fear for their safety perhaps if  they're out of state somewhere else that  there'd be a little less uh you know  relative sensitivity to the issue so  between that and then being less safe uh  the prisons being considered more safe  on the federal side they would prefer to  be on the federal side as opposed to the  state where your criminals are a lot  more violent julie somebody could look  at this and say they were already locked  away from in life in prison so what do  these sentences mean  considering they already had life in  prison on the state charges  you're right not in a sense not much  because they're already  at least with regards to travis  mcmichael and his father they're already  serving life sentences in state court so  they're never getting out no matter what  whereas with uh roddy roddy brian it's a  little different uh because he his  sentence was um there was a potential  for parole in his state case um the one  thing it does though is keep in mind  they're going to appeal and if they are  not successful with their state appeal  keep in mind they still have to appeal  the federal case so it's going to be  tough to get two bites of the apple in  terms of trying to get um their cases  disposed of in a way that's positive for  them so at least these guilty these  sentences make that much more difficult  for for each of the defendants julie  were you surprised 35 years for brian  i i wasn't i think it was important for  the judge to distinguish the behavior  between each of the three men and and  show the sentences that were appropriate  for those that pulled the trigger versus  that individual that participated but  potentially had less culpability uh than  the two major players yeah it's  definitely a major development in a  story that first started with a cell  phone video and has worked its way  through state court and now federal  court and it's just incredible to see  how it started and where it ultimately  ended up  new mexico investigators say a fourth  murder of a muslim man may be connected  to three other similar killings  officials in albuquerque announced their  press conference over the weekend that  they have reason to believe that the  killing of a man last week is related to  killings of other muslim men in the area  two members 