 the  penalty phase trial for the gunman takes  a break a break this week  fourteen-year-old gina montalto was one  of the 17 students and staff members who  was killed on valentine's day in 2018 at  marjory stoneman douglas high school her  father tony started an organization in  her name after the mass shooting to help  students with the cost of post-secondary  education and make charitable donations  to causes his daughter supported tony  testified at the penalty phase trial  wearing the suit that he wore to the  last father-daughter dance he attended  with gina as i sat down with him he told  me what he what needs to happen to make  schools safer  i think that  [Music]  but the idea of doing nothing is  unacceptable the idea of  you know  well it's not going to be perfect so we  won't do anything  we have to stop that thing we have to  come together as an american family  if you go into a room full of  republicans and ask who wants safe  schools everybody raises their hands go  in a room full of democrats and ask that  same question everyone raises their  hands we have to start looking at what  unites us here  you know we are the united states of  america  and we have to stop uh bickering with  the far left and the far right and start  coming together as an american family to  make our students and teachers safer at  school  so joe when i spoke to tony he walked me  through their efforts for the last four  years to make changes on the legislative  front and it was amazing to hear  everything that they've been doing but  as i was listening and i wanted to talk  to you about it  do you think that we can be in a  position to ensure that there's not  another parkland or sandy hook or uvalde  in the future  we couldn't totally insure it in any  event can we make it less likely so that  question becomes in turn  what about the conditions that created  this instance are they more likely to  continue to occur there's a mental  health element and people recognize that  and that's out there and maybe in most  in recent legislation that's been  addressed somewhat there's a idea of  school safety evaluated there's a  argument that some other things could  have been done in that regard by the  police who did nothing  but at the end of the day it's about the  culture of violence and the ease of  purchase of firearms you still have to  deal with that one way or the other and  without dealing with that i think that  that's the single most important element  that's going to help along with the  others to hopefully get to a place to  make this less likely and what was  interesting about speaking with tony is  he focused so much attention not even on  the gun control debate but more so  finding the warning signs which it  seemed that both sides could agree on  that that was a common factor in all of  these shootings now julie just going  back to the trial for a second  there is this two-week delay and i'm  curious this two-week delay before the  defense begins is that an advantage to  them  let me be clear when i say whether they  put it over for two weeks two months two  years 20 years the jury  has been impacted permanently for what  they've heard already from the victims  and the family members of the  individuals that passed away and were  injured so i don't think it really at  the end of the day matters does it give  them a little more time to cross their  t's and dot their eyes for things  perhaps they missed uh when they were  when the prosecution was going through  the case of course but do i think it's  going to matter in any way shape or form  to this jury when they're listening uh  to the mitigation absolutely absolutely  not  it is an uphill battle for this defense  to try to save the life of their client  after everything this jury has seen  joe julie thank you so much really  appreciate it and everyone out there  thanks for joining us here on law and  crime daily we'll see you next time as  we discuss justice in america  [Music